LNG-0001 — SLeeLa JVM-family language ingest ("langin")
=======================================================

Status:    Normative for the langin ingest surface
Component: impl/langin
Compiler:  Sleelvac(TM) 1.4
Copyright: MEARVK LLC 2026


1. Purpose
----------
langin lets SLeeLa accept the **JVM language family** as modern compiled input
types. These five "brother languages" all compile to JVM bytecode and share one
runtime, so SLeeLa treats them as one ingest family:

    Java     (.java)          the foundation of the ecosystem
    Kotlin   (.kt, .kts)      pragmatic, null-safe, 100% Java-interop
    Scala    (.scala, .sc)    object-oriented + advanced functional
    Groovy   (.groovy, .gvy)  flexible/dynamic; drives the Gradle build system
    Clojure  (.clj, .cljs,    a Lisp dialect; data-as-code, pure-functional
             .cljc)

langin ingests a source unit's **class skeleton** and reconstructs an equivalent,
runnable `sleela::Program`, exactly the way the `.xclass` loader (XCI-0001)
ingests SecureJDK 28 XML class files. From the reconstructed Program it can emit
Sleela source (`.sleela`) and/or an `.xclass` structural descriptor.


2. Scope: a STRUCTURAL transpile (skeleton only)
------------------------------------------------
langin is intentionally a **structural** front end, NOT a source-language
compiler. It reconstructs:

  * the package / namespace,
  * the primary class / interface / object / trait / namespace name,
  * fields (name + mapped type),
  * method signatures (name, mapped return type, mapped parameter types).

It does NOT translate executable logic. Method bodies are **synthesized
skeletons** — a single default `return` for the mapped return type (mirroring the
`.xclass` loader's `synthBody`). This keeps langin dependency-free: it needs no
`javac`/`kotlinc`/`scalac`/`groovyc`/Clojure runtime, only a small hand-written
scanner per language. It is native C++ and builds on all three OSes through the
existing `impl/` toolchain.


3. Type mapping
---------------
Source-language types map onto SLeeLa's surface types
(`int`/`double`/`boolean`/`String`/`void`); every other reference type is carried
as a struct handle (`int`), consistent with the `.xclass` model.

    void, Unit, Nothing                         -> void
    int, Integer, long, short, byte             -> int
    double, float, BigDecimal                   -> double
    boolean, Bool                               -> boolean
    String, char, CharSequence                  -> String
    <any other object/reference type>           -> int   (struct handle)

Kotlin nullability (`T?`) and generic arguments (`List<T>`) are stripped to their
base type before mapping.


4. Per-language recognition (best-effort skeleton scan)
-------------------------------------------------------
Java / Groovy : `[modifiers] RetType name(params) {|;`  for methods;
                `[modifiers] Type name ;|=`             for fields.
Kotlin        : `fun name(params): RetType`             for methods;
                `val|var name: Type`                    for fields.
Scala         : `def name(params): RetType`             for methods;
                `val|var name: Type`                    for fields.
Clojure       : `(ns a.b.c)` -> namespace; `(defn f [args] ...)` -> methods;
                `(def name ...)` -> fields. The namespace is sanitized into a
                Sleela identifier for the reconstructed class name.

Parameter lists accept both Java-style `Type name` and Kotlin/Scala-style
`name: Type`; default values are dropped.


5. CLI
------
    sleela langin [--run|--emit-sleela|--emit-xclass|--info] <file...>

      --run          ingest + compile + run the reconstructed Program (default)
      --emit-sleela  print the reconstructed Sleela source (alias: --emit)
      --emit-xclass  print an .xclass structural descriptor (compiler="langin")
      --info         print a one-line summary per ingested unit

A JVM-family file also runs by extension via `sleela run <file>` or the bare
`sleela <file>` form. As with every other execution path, the SHA-256 execution
gate (`SLEELA_SHA256_MANIFEST`) applies.

If no ingested unit defines `void main()`, langin synthesizes a `LangInMain`
entry shim that prints the ingest summary, so the reconstructed Program always
runs.


6. Data-integrity note
----------------------
langin reports only what it can structurally recover from the source text. It is
honest about being skeleton-only: an emitted `.sleela`/`.xclass` preserves the
type contract and structure of the input, not its behavior. Anything it cannot
recognize is omitted rather than guessed.
